ts page is titled with the server's name, and an operator matching journal to page sees two different names. tests/test_config.py::TestThePlayersName pins only the local value. Scope: instance. Either derive the startup line's name through client.player_name (the cached .client.json is present at start), with a test that has a cached document naming the Player differently from PLAYER_NAME, or record that the startup line deliberately reports the local key.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "tests/test_config.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "UiSettings.settings_path is built and never read", "recommendation": "Built-but-unconsumed. Nothing in src/, tests/ or tools/ reads `settings_path` (searched: grep -rn settings_path). Every caller goes through store.path_in or store.read directly. A maintainer may take it to be the path the page writes through and change it to no effect. Scope: instance. Delete the property, or route the store's callers through it.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Security headers are missing from aiohttp's raised error responses (404, 405), though security-model.md says every response carries them", "recommendation": "server._headers updates the headers only on a response that the handler returns. HTTPNotFound and HTTPMethodNotAllowed, which aiohttp raises for unknown paths (GET /nope, GET /settings) and for refused static paths, propagate past the middleware without CSP, X-Frame-Options or nosniff. They carry nothing secret, so this is not exploitable. But the security-model.md bullets say 'Every response sets a CSP' and 'Every response also sets\u2026', and test_every_answer_carries_the_security_headers covers only four routed paths. Scope: instance. Either catch HTTPException in _headers, apply the headers to it and re-raise or return it, and add an unknown path and a wrong-method path to the test's parameters; or narrow the claim to the page's own answers.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py", "tests/test_settings_ui.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The startup line's player_name is the local fallback, though .env.example says the server's name wins there", "recommendation": "Instance. `.env.example` (PLAYER_NAME) says the name is 'shown on the settings page and in the startup line. Once a server has given this client a name, the server's wins', and the plan's Goals say the same. The page does this through `client.player_name(cache_dir, local)`. `ClientSettings.startup_lines()` logs `self.player_name` instead, which is only PLAYER_NAME. So a paired Player whose server names it logs `player_name: (none \u2014 PLAYER_NAME is not set)` at `client.started`, and an operator reading the journal sees the wrong name, or none. Two things share the name `player_name`: the effective name (`client.player_name`) and the local fallback (`ClientSettings.player_name`). Either log the effective name, derived at the point the startup line is emitted (`client.Supervisor` can call `player_name(cache_dir, settings.player_name)`), or rename the field and the startup key to say they are the local fallback (e.g. `local_player_name`) and correct the .env.example sentence. One way or the other, the line and the prose have to agree.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/client.py", ".env.example"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The rule that the environment wins over the page is worked out twice, and the copies already normalise differently", "recommendation": "Class: every reader of a page-managed key's effective value. `config.load` merges with `_beneath_the_environment`, stripping PLAYER_NAME and right-stripping '/' from SERVER_URL. The page separately computes `UiSettings.pinned` / `pinned_values` in `load_ui` and chooses between them in `server._shown`, and it shows the raw environment value. So a pinned `SERVER_URL=http://x:8770/` or ` Hall ` is shown on the page differently from what the Player uses. Today the two copies agree on truthiness, but nothing holds them together. The construction is for `load_ui` to take its effective values from the same merge, and its normalisation, that `load` uses (one owner of 'env beats file'), so that the page shows exactly what the Player runs with.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "UiSettings.settings_path has no consumer", "recommendation": "Instance. Nothing in src/ or tests/ reads `UiSettings.settings_path`. Every reader goes through `store.path_in` / `store.read(cache_dir)`. Delete the property.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "server_url_problem binds `port` only to `del` it", "recommendation": "Instance. `port = parts.port` inside the try and `del port` before the return exist only to make urlsplit's lazy port parsing raise ValueError. A reader would take `del port` for leftover code. `parts.port  # raises ValueError for a port that is not a number or is out of range` as a bare expression in the try says the same thing without the dead binding. No behaviour change.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "deploy/README's new section cites the plan by path", "recommendation": "Instance, and an existing house convention: the earlier sections' `build-plan-clients.md` and `build-plan-displays-and-label-outputs.md` already point at plans that are no longer under .prawduct/artifacts, and deploy/README's own note near line 331 says why. `## The settings page (2026-11-10, `build-plan-settings-ui.md`)` will dangle the same way once this plan is archived. Cite it by scope (`settings-ui`) or by the change record `.prawduct/changes/settings-ui.md`. Nothing reads this text, so changing it is optional.", "files": ["deploy/README.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "The login throttle's failure counter is unbounded, and its 2.0 ** (failures - 1) overflows after 1,025 heard wrong attempts, locking the owner out until a restart", "recommendation": "Instance. `auth.Throttle.failed` computes `min(2.0 ** (self.failures - 1), MAXIMUM_THROTTLE_SECONDS)` and nothing bounds `failures`. Python float pow raises `OverflowError: (34, 'Result too large')` at 2.0 ** 1024 (checked in an interpreter). So the 1,025th heard failure raises. After that, `_login` and `_change_password` charge the throttle before they check the password, so every later attempt, the owner's right password included, is a 500 until the process restarts. At the 60 s cap an attacker on the LAN reaches that point in about 17 hours of one guess a minute. That is the abuse case the security model's table lists ('Guess the page's password'), and the throttle turns it into a lockout. Fix: clamp the exponent, for example `min(self.failures - 1, 6)`, or stop incrementing once the cap is reached. Pin it with a test that drives `failed()` past 1,100 calls with an injected clock and then checks that a right password still succeeds after the wait. It also falls under the core rule 'When you fix a defect, make inputs the fix does not know fail by name': the throttle is tested only at small counts (`TestTheThrottle`).", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/auth.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A failed settings write in /setup spends the setup code first, so every retry reads 'That setup link is not this start's'", "recommendation": "Instance, with two sibling sites. `_setup` sets `state.setup_code = None` before `store.write`. `store.write` documents that it raises `OSError`, and its own comment says a first start in setup mode may be what creates `CACHE_DIR`, which is the likeliest moment for a permission or disk failure. When the write fails, the person gets a bare 500. The page now has no password and no code, so retrying the same link gets `_bad_code`'s 'not this start's' message, which is false. The only way out is a restart, and nothing on the page says so. `_save` and `_change_password` call `store.write` with no handling either. They lose no state, but they answer with a bare 500 and leave no `settings_page.*` event. The only trace is aiohttp's generic 'Error handling request'. Fix: catch `OSError` around the write. In `_setup`, restore the code, or keep it spent and say a restart is needed, and log a named event (for example `settings_page.write_failed`) carrying the path and the errno. Answer all three sites with a page that names the file. Test it by making `cache_dir` unwritable or by patching `store.write` to raise. No test currently drives a write failure through the page.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/settings_ui/server.py", "src/arrt_player/settings_ui/store.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: 'When code is added, moved or retired, ask which guards were scoped to the old shape' \u2014 tools/tv_api_check.py still catches only ConfigError, and a missing server now raises SetupRequired", "recommendation": "Before this change, a missing `SERVER_URL` or `CLIENT_TOKEN` raised `ConfigError` from `config.load`. `tools/tv_api_check.py:main` catches that and prints 'Cannot check the television: \u2026' with exit 2. The change makes the same condition raise `SetupRequired`, deliberately not a `ConfigError` ('so a handler for one can never quietly take the other'). The tool's handler was scoped to the old shape, so on a Player in setup mode it now ends in a traceback. `power_probe.py` and `label_preview.py` catch `Exception` and are unaffected. The suite is silent because nothing imports the tool, which is the core rule 'When you delete a constant or add a required parameter, grep `tools/` and `scripts/` for consumers'. Instance fix: catch `SetupRequired` beside `ConfigError` in `tv_api_check.main`. That tool needs only the Frame settings, so it could also read them without requiring the server keys. Mechanising the rule would take a test parametrised over every `tools/*.py` that calls `config.load`, run with an environment that lacks `SERVER_URL`, asserting a one-line refusal with no traceback. That would make the next new exception type from `load` fail by name.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py", "tools/tv_api_check.py"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "UiSettings.settings_path is built and never read", "recommendation": "Instance (scope-trace). `config.UiSettings.settings_path` (config.py:300) has no caller in src/, tests/ or tools/; `grep -rn settings_path` returns only its definition. Every consumer calls `store.path_in`/`store.read(ui.cache_dir)` directly. Delete it, or have the page's error messages use it. It is a two-line property, so NOTE.", "files": ["src/arrt_player/config.py"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Plan goals still marked inferred: the page's default bind to every interface, the new start-up refusals, environment-over-page precedence, cookie and throttle parameters, and manual password recovery", "recommendation": "Instance. `build-plan-settings-ui.md` \u00a7 Goals still marks these *Inferred*: `UI_BIND` defaulting to `0.0.0.0` with port 8771; the environment winning over the page; the cookie's 30 days, HttpOnly/SameSite and throttle; and recovery by hand-editing `auth.password`. The new `config.load` validation of `SERVER_URL`, `CLIENT_TOKEN` and `PLAYER_NAME` is marked 'Not settled by the goals'. The change record's 'Confirmed by the owner' list covers none of them. Two have consequences beyond